' ── DECIDED 2026-11-14: THE STOPGAP (the owner: "Stopgap now") ────────────
' The owner chose to keep the per-file copy and fix it in place, shipped as
' its own PR from main. The long-term direction (claude's session dirs on a
' live host bind instead of volumes) is a separate plan. Evidence: the state
' round-trip revision (findings F2-F6, option A) and its adversarial check
' (corrections R1-R11, the corrected stopgap), both 2026-11-14, with
' reproductions that source this lib in debian:13-slim (enospc.sh, sim.sh,
' xws.sh). A second adversarial check of the stopgap (defects D1-D8, same
' day) found two paths that still lost history and two fix lines that sent
' the operator nowhere; each D below names its fix and its pin.
'
'   F2  A FAILED SAVE REMOVED THE SANDBOX ANYWAY. MEASURED in code: teardown
'       printed "⚠ resume state — not saved" and went on to `sbx rm`, and
'       retireSandbox did the same. The 2026-09-01 loss of a four-day session
'       (proveo-1787956302-22788, the -w / section below) had this shape.
'       NOW: normalEndOf(forced, stranded, unsaved, retainsEngine) returns
'       endKeepUnsaved, which closes on "⚠ <name> — kept: its resume state
'       did not reach the host" and the fix "the next run re-attaches and
'       saves it again; `proveo rm [--stop] <name>` saves it, then removes
'       it; `sbx rm --force <name>` deletes it with the state only in it".
'       Stranded work outranks it and adds "its resume state did not reach
'       the host either". retireSandbox returns the same reason as an error
'       and runs no `rm`, as a stranded clone does; its fix starts "the next
'       run retries the save", since the next run retires a stale sandbox
'       again rather than re-attaching it (D4). A forced remove chosen in
'       the Ctrl+C dialog still removes.
'       D1: SaveState skipped the save, as a success, whenever sbx.Exists
'       said no, and `sbx ls` can exit 0 with a header-only table under
'       load, so the sandbox was then removed with its state. Teardown and retire now skip only when sbx.Gone proves it
'       gone, which reads a header-only table again as `sbx ls --json`; a
'       listing that proves nothing runs the save, and a failed save keeps.
'       D2: the save swallowed the configuration save's failure, and codex
'       and cursor history, and Claude's ~/.claude files outside the volumes
'       (history.jsonl, file-history, plans), travel only by config sync.
'       SaveStateArgs now fails when the config or the state save fails;
'       only the toolchain save stays `|| true`. It calls only functions
'       every older lib defines.
'       Pinned: TestANormalEndRemovesTheSandboxUnlessWorkStateOrTheEngineStaysInIt,
'       TestAResumeStateThatDidNotSaveKeepsTheSandboxWithItsFix,
'       TestAnOperatorChosenRemovalStillRemovesASandboxWhoseStateDidNotSave,
'       TestStrandedWorkAndUnsavedStateSayBothOnTheKeptLine,
'       TestARetireWhoseResumeStateDidNotSaveKeepsTheSandbox,
'       TestARetireWhoseResumeStateSavedRemovesTheSandbox,
'       TestAnEmptySbxTableNeitherSkipsTheSaveNorRemovesTheSandbox (D1),
'       TestTheSaveFailsWhenConfigOrStateDidNotReachTheHost (D2).
'   F3  A RESTORE INTO A FULL VOLUME TRUNCATED THE HOST AT THE NEXT SAVE.
'       MEASURED (enospc.sh, a 2 MiB tmpfs): restore rc=1 with 2,097,152
'       bytes in the volume, then the save cut the host file from 4,000,001
