'       TestTheGuardedSaveSkipsAnImageOlderThanTheRestoreWait.
'   F7  GROWTH ONLY. Every create gets fresh volumes, and each used to receive
'       the whole store (about 1.3 GB). The projects scope cuts that to this
'       workspace's entries (351 MB for the largest key measured). Decision 3
'       then closed the leak itself: the kit declares no volume.
' The new rules live in the image's lib: they reach a sandbox only through
' `mise run build <harness>`, and a sandbox an older image created keeps the
' older save for as long as it lives.
'
' ── THE PROJECTS/ KEY (F5) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
' MEASURED in the Claude Code 2.1.323 bundle: transcripts live in
' projects/<nH(cwd)>, where nH(p) is p.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/g,"-") and, past
' 200 units, the first 200 plus "-" plus Math.abs(hash(p)).toString(36). The
' auto-memory dir keys on the canonical git root (a linked worktree's main
' worktree), not on the cwd. Host store names have that shape, e.g.
' -Users-dev-Projects-app--claude-worktrees-feat-x for a linked worktree.
' D8: this example and the test rows use a neutral home; the first draft
' quoted the operator's own (spec-conventions.puml pins the rule).
' _proveo_claude_project_dirs restores the entries named for PROVEO_WORKDIR
' (agentWorkdir, the agent's cwd), its physical path, every path `git
' worktree list --porcelain` prints for its repository, and the root `git
' rev-parse --git-common-dir` names. A key over 200 units matches on its
' 200-unit prefix, since the hash is not reproduced. A non-ASCII path encodes
' per UTF-16 unit: é is one '-', an astral character two.
' Restoring the memory key matters: save is newer-wins, so a MEMORY.md the
' agent wrote fresh beside an unrestored one would replace the host's.
' D5, MEASURED in the 2.1.323 bundle: `claude --resume` lists and resumes
' the sessions of every worktree of the repository. The bundle runs `git
' worktree list --porcelain` and loads each worktree's sessions, and a resume
' by id logs tengu_resume_worktree_fallback. Hence the worktree paths above:
' without them a --clone=false run, whose guest reads the host's .git, lost
' its sibling worktrees' sessions from --resume. [Inference] In clone mode
' the guest lists only the main checkout and this worktree, which the
' restore covered already.
' todos/ keeps the whole-store scope (owner, 2026-11-14); sessions/ no
' longer travels (decision 2).
' Pinned: TestClaudeProjectKeyMatchesClaudeCode,
' TestStateRestoreCopiesOnlyThisWorkspacesProjects.
'
' ── SAVE WAITS FOR THIS BOOT'S RESTORE (F3) ───────────────────────────────
' A teardown, retire or rm exec on a stopped sandbox boots it, and the seed's
' restore then runs beside the exec's save (the collision section below).
' Save waits up to 60s, holding no lock, until the restore marker, the seed's
' release marker (PROVEO_INSTRUCTIONS_MARKER, written after the restore), or
' its refused or halted marker exists; then it requires the restore marker. A
' sandbox whose state dirs hold nothing to save never waits, which is every
' codex, cursor, opencode and hermes sandbox: their manifests declare no
' state: dirs.
' Pinned: TestStateSaveRefusesWithoutThisBootsRestore (a marker that appears
' while the save waits), TestSeedRestoresStateBeforeItReleasesTheAgent (the
' order the wait relies on).
' [Unverified] That an `sbx exec` sees the /dev/shm the seed wrote its marker
' in; if it does not, every claudecode save refuses. [Inference] They share
' it, since `sbx exec` reads the agent's /proc; an e2e run confirms it.
'
' ── NO DESTINATION NAME IS WRITTEN IN PLACE (F3) ──────────────────────────
' `cp` opens its destination with O_TRUNC and streams into it, so an
' interrupted, concurrent or out-of-space copy leaves the destination cut at
' a buffer boundary, and the cut file then looks newest (cp sets the source
' mtime only after the data). _proveo_state_tree therefore:
'   1. new files and links: one bulk `cp -a --parents` into a staging dir
'      inside the destination (.proveo-sync.<pid>.XXXXXX, same filesystem),
'      then `mv -u -t` per directory, its names fed through `xargs -0` so a
'      directory past the exec argument limit still lands (D3). After a
'      failed copy, only a staged file whose size equals the planned size is
'      placed. Batching per directory keeps the restore off a fork per file:
'      measured 5000 shared files cost 4.6s through a fork-per-file copy
'      against 0.067s for one bulk cp, and the restore races the agent's 30s
'      gate.
'   2. replaced files: copied to <name>.proveo-sync.<pid>.<random> beside the
'      target and renamed over it; rename(2) is atomic.
' Files only the destination has stay. A sync has never deleted: the
' operator's home holds sessions this sandbox was never given. The plan
' prunes *.proveo-sync.* names, so debris a killed sync left never crosses.
'
' ── THE SAME-TICK WINDOW, NOW SAVE-ONLY (F4) ──────────────────────────────
' cp -a preserves size and mtime, so equal stamps normally mean the copy
' already happened. Not always: on the sandbox's overlay two files written in
' one clock tick report the same nanosecond mtime (measured, both sides
' 1787846280.6672317090, same size, different content). A save therefore
' copies a file the guest wrote in the last 5s unless the host's is newer;
' those are the transcripts the agent just wrote. A restore takes no window:
' on a tie the guest keeps its copy, and keeping a copy never reverts a turn.
'
' ── ACCEPTED LIMIT: CLOCK SKEW (D7) ───────────────────────────────────────
' Newer-wins trusts the two clocks. MEASURED: with the host copy stamped 10
' minutes ahead, guest turns written 30s and 0s before the save never reach
' the host, and the save still returns 0, so teardown removes the sandbox.
' The same skew lets a restore replace a kept guest's unsaved turn. Equal
' stamps with different sizes behave alike: a save skips a guest file last
' written more than 5s before it. origin/main copied on any difference and
' had none of this; it had F4 and F5 instead. The revision named skew as
' option A's risk, and the stopgap accepts it. [Inference] The VM's clock
' follows the host's, so skew needs a host clock step or a store another
' host wrote. [Inference] The separate plan's live host bind retires the
' copy, and this limit with it.
'
' ── TWO COPY ENGINES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' _proveo_sync_tree with _proveo_changed_files stays the toolchain sync's
' engine, where the source wins on any size or mtime difference: npm writes
' every package file with one fixed mtime, so two versions of a file can tie
' and a newer-wins rule would keep the stale one. The state sync runs on
' _proveo_state_tree. e2e/sync_state_test.go runs its tree probes on both.
'
' ── CORRECTED 2026-11-14: `sbx rm` KEEPS KIT VOLUMES SINCE v0.50 ──────────
' This diagram said `sbx rm --force` removes "VM + images + volumes". That
' held before sbx v0.50.0. Its release notes: "Persistent kit volumes retain
' data across kit updates and sandbox removal and recreation with the same
' name", for sandboxes created with that version; `sbx rm --help` (v0.50.0)
' says creating the same sandbox name with the same kit reattaches. proveo
' writes each run's kit to a new dir (sandbox.go, KitDir under RunDir), so the
' volumes stay behind and no later run reattaches them (F7): the host copy is
' still the only one a later run sees, which is why the copy-out exists.
' Other specs still carry the old sentence (F17, not in this change):
' proveohome/proveo-home-lifecycle.puml, _experiments/docker-sandbox.puml.
' SUPERSEDED by decision 3: the dirs were discovered from /proc/mounts ("a
' block device under $HOME is sandbox-local by definition"), with statsig
' and shell-snapshots skipped. The kit mounts none now; the manifest's
' state: list names the dirs, and transient: keeps the scratch in the VM.
' The discovery survives only as row C's fallback, which skips sessions too.
'
' ── THE COLLISION, MEASURED (2026-09) ─────────────────────────────────────
' The failure handler copied state out before it looked for a transcript, and
' on a stopped sandbox `sbx exec` started the VM to do it, so the kit's
' startup seed was still inside restore when the exec's save began: two
' copies, opposite directions, same files. Mounts re-evaluated 15:12:13.030;
' the colliding writes landed 15:12:13.62-.69. Seven transcripts were
' rewritten short in that second (7340032, 786432, 786432, 524288, 262144,
' 262144 and 262144 bytes, every size a 256 KiB multiple, each cut mid-JSON),
' and the short copy then propagated, so no intact copy remained. Nothing
' reported it: the copy sent stderr to /dev/null and ended in `|| true`. The
' lock serialises the two since; the restore marker makes the save wait.
'
' ── THE LOCK IS mkdir, NOT flock ──────────────────────────────────────────
' mkdir is atomic on every filesystem in play and needs no util-linux in the
' image. Both callers live in one PID namespace, so the pid recorded in the
' lock settles whether a holder is alive: a seed killed mid-copy leaves
' debris, and debris that blocked every later run forever would be worse than
' the failure it fixes. Waiting caps at 60s, an order of magnitude over any
' measured sync.
'
' ── AN HONEST STATUS AND A TOLERANT SEED ──────────────────────────────────
' e2e/sync_state_test.go reads the destination's SIZE while a copy runs. With
' a rename into place only two sizes can show, the old and the new; with a
' streaming overwrite the samples land in between. It also asserts the copy
' happened, since a no-op leaves no partial file either. proveo_sync_state
' returns a real status, so save's caller and the tests see a partial copy,
' while the seed calls it as `proveo_sync_state restore || true`: proveo-seed
' runs under set -euo pipefail, and one failed file copy once aborted the
' kit's startup command ("failed to run sandbox container"). The failure is
' still said on stderr (TestSeedSurvivesAFailedStateSync).
'
' ── WHY THE TEARDOWN EXEC PINS `-w /` ─────────────────────────────────────
' Without it the exec inherits the container's WorkingDir — the first workspace
' — and on this backend that directory can stop resolving while the sandbox
' lives on (_spec/internal/sbx/virtiofs-cwd-invalidation.puml). The runtime
' then fails at chdir before a byte is copied — `OCI runtime exec failed:
' getcwd: Operation not permitted`, exit 127 — teardown reports "resume state
' not preserved" and goes on to remove the volumes. That is how a four-day
' session's transcripts were lost on proveo-1787956302-22788.
'
' The sync itself never reads the cwd: the lib is sourced by absolute path and
' every directory it moves comes from $HOME and PROVEO_STATE_HOME. Reproduced
' on demand by replacing the workspace directory's inode on the host (mv +
' mkdir); putting the directory back does not heal the guest. `/` is the one
' directory every container has and every user may enter.
'
' The host side of the copy is named by StateHomeVar in the sandbox
' environment, never handed over again on the argv.
'
' ── THE TOOLCHAIN STAMP (2026-10-13) ───────────────────────────────────────
' proveo_sync_tools keeps a token BESIDE each tree, never inside it:
' <state>/toolchains/.<platform>.proveo-sync and the same beside the agent's
' tree. A save removes the store's token before copying and writes a fresh one
' to both sides only after a clean copy, so a half-done save never vouches for
' itself. A restore whose store token equals the home token returns before
' walking the tree; a missing token, a mismatch or a failed copy means a full
' sync, and a failed restore leaves no home token. Measured: a replayed
' restore re-stamped 9,697 directories of a 66,552-entry tree in ~8s. `proveo
' clean` globs toolchains/* for DIRECTORIES only, and the dot-name hides the
' token from the glob anyway. Pinned by TestToolRestoreSkipsAStoreTheHomeAlreadyHolds.
'
' A save never runs while this boot is still provisioning. The background
' provision step writes its pid to /dev/shm/proveo-provisioning and removes it
' when done; proveo_sync_tools save skips (and says so) while that pid lives.
' Without it, a one-shot run ending 40s in would copy a half-installed mise or
' npm tree to the store and stamp it as a clean save; the next VM's `command -v`
' gates would then trust it. A dead pid does not block: a killed step leaves
' only debris. Pinned by TestToolSaveSkipsATreeStillBeingProvisioned.
'
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title State sync — resume state between the operator's home and the\nsandbox's own disk: no partial file, no reverted turn, no lost sandbox, no VM-local path

node "host" as HOST <<host>> {
  database "PROVEO_HOME/.claude\nprojects · todos\nthe copy every later run sees" as MOUNT <<db>>
  database "PROVEO_HOME/.claude\nsessions · daemon · jobs …\nleft as they are" as LEFT <<db>>
}

component "harness.manifest\nstate: [projects, todos]\ntransient: [sessions, …]" as MAN <<app>>

node "sandbox" as BOX <<app>> {
  component "proveo_seed\nkit setup.startup" as SEED <<app>>
  component "proveo_sync_state restore\nhost → VM disk\nprojects/: this run's keys" as RESTORE <<app>>
  component "proveo_sync_state save\nVM disk → host\nafter this boot's restore" as SAVE <<async>>
  database "/dev/shm\nproveo-state-restored" as MARK <<db>>
  component "_proveo_sync_lock\nmkdir, pid-checked" as LOCK <<app>>
  component "_proveo_state_tree\n1. new: stage + rename\n2. newer: temp + rename" as TREE <<app>>
  database "image home, plain dirs\n$HOME/.claude/projects · todos\nno volume@1" as VOL <<db>>
  database "VM-local\n$HOME/.claude/sessions · statsig\nshell-snapshots · daemon* · jobs" as LOCAL <<db>>
}

component "teardown · retire · proveo rm\nSaveState → sbx exec -w /" as FAIL <<error>>
component "⚠ kept: its resume state\ndid not reach the host" as KEEP <<error>>

SEED ARROW_MAIN RESTORE : before the release
RESTORE ARROW_MAIN MARK : only a clean restore
SAVE ARROW_OPTIONAL MARK : absent → refuse
FAIL ARROW_ERROR SAVE : a stopped sandbox boots…
FAIL ARROW_ERROR SEED : …and its seed restores
FAIL ARROW_ERROR KEEP : save failed, not forced
RESTORE ARROW_MAIN LOCK
SAVE ARROW_MAIN LOCK
LOCK ARROW_MAIN TREE : one direction at a time
TREE ARROW_MAIN VOL : restore: host newer
TREE ARROW_QUEUE MOUNT : save: guest newer
MOUNT ARROW_OPTIONAL RESTORE : yesterday's sessions
MAN ARROW_MAIN RESTORE : PROVEO_STATE_DIRS
MAN ARROW_OPTIONAL LOCAL : config sync skips both ways
LOCAL ARROW_ERROR LEFT : never copied

note right of KEEP
  **F2: a failed save keeps the sandbox.** The
  next run saves again; ""proveo rm"" saves,
  then removes; ""sbx rm --force"" deletes it
  unsaved. The Ctrl+C dialog's forced remove
  still deletes past a failed save.
end note

note right of RESTORE
  **F4/F5: restore takes only what the host
  holds newer**, and in projects/ only this
  run's keys: the workdir's and the repository
  root's, where Claude Code keeps its memory.
end note

note left of SAVE
  **F3: save needs this boot's restore.** It
  waits up to 60s for the seed, then refuses,
  non-zero and on stderr, with no marker. A
  state dir with nothing to save never waits.
end note

note bottom of TREE
  **F3: no name is written in place.** New
  files stage inside the destination and are
  renamed in; a replaced file goes through a
  temp name. A cut copy never takes a name.
end note

note bottom of LOCK
  **""mkdir"", not ""flock"".** Atomic on every
  filesystem in play, and the recorded pid
  breaks a dead holder's lock. Waits cap at 60s.
end note

note right of VOL
  **No volume since decision 3.** The vendored
  kit stranded five per run (1,710 records);
  the manifest's state: list names the dirs.
end note

note right of LOCAL
  **Decision 2: VM-local.** sessions/ holds
  per-VM pids no VM can tell apart; transient
  keeps these in, and the host's copy as is.
end note

note right of FAIL
  **The collision, measured:** an exec booted
  a stopped VM, and restore and save hit the
  same files; seven transcripts were cut short.
  Save now waits for the restore marker.
end note
@enduml
